evidence:
backup.go:57-75: the handler accepts `Content string `json:"content"`` and when no ID is given uses it verbatim: `} else {
		data = req.Content
	}` — LoadBundleJSON (runtime/bundle.go:171-180) only does `json.Unmarshal` with no allowlist, no schema check and no provenance check. RestoreBundle then writes every settings key blindly: bundle.go:136-137 `for k, v := range b.Settings {` / `if err := tx.Save(&model.SystemSetting{Key: k, ValueJSON: v}).Error; err != nil {` — including `general` (settings/settings.go:20 `GuardEnabled          bool   `json:"guard_enabled"`` and lines 25-27 `CallAuditEnabled    bool `json:"call_audit_enabled"`` …), `security` (settings.go:79-84 `type Security struct {
	MFAEnabled        bool `json:"mfa_enabled"``), and `quota_alerts` (settings.go:70 `WebhookURL string `json:"webhook_url"``). Export deliberately skips only one key on the way OUT (bundle.go:61 `if s.Key == model.SetKeyConfigMeta { // 恢复时保留新的 meta，不覆盖`) but the restore path has NO equivalent filter, so even config_meta can be injected. Restore also wipes 8 tables (bundle.go:81-89) and re-seeds quotas with stale UsedValue (bundle.go:126-128), rewinding consumption recorded since the backup.

impact:
A single authenticated POST (no confirmation, no re-auth even for MFA-bound accounts) can simultaneously: delete the entire keyword/PII/injection rule set, set guard_enabled=false, turn call auditing off (gateway/gateway.go:253-256 `if !g.CallAuditEnabled {
		return false
	}` then errors are no longer written either), force global MFA off (auth.go:86 `if sec.MFAEnabled && u.MFAEnabled {` gating), shrink audit_retention_days so audit.cleanupLoop (audit/audit.go:88-106) deletes history, and point quota_alerts.webhook_url at an attacker host that the server then POSTs to (quota/quota.go:222 `go postWebhook(s.WebhookURL, payload)` — an exfil/SSRF channel). A 'restored config' also silently re-arms quota usage backwards. While each individual change is admin-privileged anyway, the restore path collapses a full security downgrade into one no-trace, no-validation, file-shaped operation.

evidence:
jwt.go:21 `ExpiresAt: jwt.NewNumericDate(time.Now().Add(24 * time.Hour)),` with HS256-only Parse (jwt.go:30-35). No revocation exists anywhere (grep for revoke/blacklist/jti across backend/internal → no matches). logout is a log line only: auth.go:152-154 `func (s *Server) logout(c *gin.Context) {
	s.recordOp(c, "login", "auth", "logout", nil, nil)`; changePassword re-hashes but keeps sessions alive (auth.go:180-181 `hash, _ := bcrypt.GenerateFromPassword(...)
	s.db.Model(&model.AdminUser{}).Where("id = ?", u.ID).Update("password_hash", string(hash))` with no token-version field on AdminUser, model.go:13-26). The same token is then transmitted in a URL: frontend/src/hooks/useAuditStream.ts:65 `const url = `${proto}//${window.location.host}/api/admin/v1/audit/ws?token=${encodeURIComponent(token)}`;` and auditws.go:31 `claims, err := auth.Parse(s.secret, c.Query("token"))`.

createProvider/updateProvider only check non-emptiness (providers.go:56 `if err := c.ShouldBindJSON(&req); err != nil || req.Name == "" || req.BaseURL == "" {`) — no scheme/host/private-range validation. testProvider then fetches it server-side: providers.go:166-168 build `base+"/models"`, line 184 `client := &http.Client{Timeout: 15 * time.Second}` (default jar and default CheckRedirect → follows up to 10 redirects into internal space), and the failure path reflects the Go error (which includes dialed/resolved IPs): providers.go:188 `s.ok(c, gin.H{"ok": false, "latency_ms": latency, "message": "连接失败: " + err.Error()})`, plus a 120-byte body excerpt via errSnippet (providers.go:374-376 `if len(s) > 120 {
